@prefix cell: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell#> .
@prefix topic: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/topic#> .
@prefix cat: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/category#> .
@prefix p: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/persona#> .
@prefix o: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/organization#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> .
@base <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell> .

<http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell/3.29.0> ;
    dc:date "2026-08-22"^^xsd:date ;
    owl:imports <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/topic> ;
    rdfs:label "Cell Ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Cell Ontology for the Mee Identity Agent. Defines cell:Cell — the self-contained content facet of a cell — independent of where any cat:Category (see category.ttl) chooses to place it in a navigational tree. cell:Cell splits further into two orthogonal sub-facets: cell:TCell (template) and cell:ACell (actual/instantiated), which a single cell individual may carry either or both of."""@en .


#################################################################
#    Annotation Properties for Cell DataBook Classification
#################################################################

cell:abstract rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:domain owl:Class ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "abstract"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "True if this class is not directly instantiated in DataBooks — it exists only to organize the class hierarchy."@en .

cell:label rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "label"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Default display name for a concrete cell:Cell subtype (e.g. \"2 Member Cell\"), asserted directly on the class rather than an instance."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell
#
#    A cell:Cell instance is a self-contained content record —
#    independent of any tree position in a category tree. A cell:Cell
#    lives inside a filesystem folder — simply whichever folder its
#    own cell-databook file physically sits in — and there is no RDF
#    individual representing that folder at all: the association is
#    purely a filesystem fact, whatever cell-databook file physically
#    sits in a folder IS that folder's content. The relationship is
#    strictly one-to-one, not many-to-one: a folder holds at most one
#    cell-databook file, whose filename must be an exact copy of the
#    folder's own name. A folder with no such matching cell-databook
#    is not a category folder at all — just a plain filesystem folder,
#    possibly holding nested category folders of its own. There is no
#    way for two cell:Cell individuals to share one folder: a cell is
#    self-contained, and letting two cells share a folder would risk a
#    single file in that folder becoming ambiguously part of both.
#
#    cell:Cell itself carries only what's common to every cell
#    regardless of facet: inherent classification (cell:origin).
#    Everything facet-specific — including cell:subject (the
#    resource(s) the cell is about) and cell:memberTopics/
#    cell:otherTopics (its linked topic:SCTopicGraph container(s))
#    — lives on cell:TCell or cell:ACell below.
#################################################################

cell:Cell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:label "Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Superclass of every cell individual, carrying only what's common regardless of facet: inherent classification (cell:origin). A cell:Cell carries no tree position of its own and no link to one — its containing folder is purely a filesystem fact, with no RDF individual representing it at all; moving a cell to a different folder is a pure filesystem operation, never anything asserted on the cell itself, which is what keeps a cell robust under PDN sync. cell:origin does not violate this: its range is the abstract classificatory cat:Category, not a tree position, so it records what kind of thing the cell is, never where it currently lives. Member composition (cell:memberCount, cell:OneMember/cell:MultiMember), creator metadata (cell:creator), identity/topic links (cell:subject, cell:memberTopics, cell:otherTopics), and validation (cell:shape) live on the cell:ACell facet, not directly here."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Facets: Template (TCell) and Actual (ACell)
#
#    A cell:Cell splits into two orthogonal facets, mirroring
#    category.ttl's Category/Folder split:
#
#    cell:TCell — the template facet. Carries cell:templateShape,
#    reachable from a cat:Category subclass via cat:templateCell
#    (category.ttl). Describes what a topic filed under that
#    category is expected to look like once instantiated.
#
#    cell:ACell — the actual/instantiated facet. Carries cell:memberCount
#    (and, by extension, cell:OneMember/cell:MultiMember), cell:creator,
#    cell:subject (required, one or two resource IRIs identifying who
#    or what the cell is about), cell:memberTopics (required, one or
#    more linked topic:SCTopicGraph containers per member in the
#    relationship — cardinality varies by member count) and
#    cell:otherTopics (any number of additional topic:SCTopicGraph
#    containers beyond that baseline), and cell:shape — the
#    sh:NodeShape(s) validating this specific cell's own content
#    directly, as opposed to content a future topic filed under a
#    template category should conform to.
#
#    A cell individual may carry either facet alone, or both at once:
#    every individual in cell-templates.ttl is both a reusable
#    template (cell:TCell, carrying cell:templateShape) and a real
#    piece of member-classified content (cell:OneMember, a cell:ACell
#    subclass, carrying cell:memberCount) — it is simply multi-typed with
#    both.
#################################################################

cell:TCell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:label "Template Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The template facet of a cell:Cell. A cell:TCell individual is reachable from a cat:Category subclass via that class's cat:templateCell value (category.ttl) — it's what Mia clones into a new cell when a category of that class is first instantiated into a user's tree (Lazy Instantiation). Carries cell:templateShape, linking to the sh:NodeShape(s) describing the content a topic filed under that category is expected to conform to. Most cell:TCell individuals carry no cell:templateShape value — that's the default, not an omission."@en .

cell:templateShape rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:TCell ;
    rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "templateShape"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a cell:TCell individual (one reached via a cat:Category subclass's cat:templateCell, category.ttl) directly to the sh:NodeShape(s) that describe the content a topic file filed under that category is expected to conform to — e.g. ctpl:PassportTemplateCell (cell-templates.ttl) carries pshapes:PassportDocumentShape (cell-templates-shacl.ttl). Makes the shape reachable by RDF traversal alone (cat:Category -> cat:templateCell -> cell:templateShape -> sh:NodeShape), not merely by file co-location or naming convention. A template cell with no known shape (most of them) carries no cell:templateShape value. Distinct from cell:shape (cell:ACell), which validates an actual cell's own content directly rather than describing what a future topic filed under a template category should look like — though when Lazy Instantiation clones this TCell into a new ACell, this value is exactly what gets copied into that clone's cell:shape."@en .

cell:ACell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:label "Actual Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The actual/instantiated facet of a cell:Cell — real, member-classified content, as opposed to reusable template content (cell:TCell). Carries cell:memberCount (and is further specialized by member count into cell:OneMember and cell:MultiMember — see Cell Member Composition below), cell:creator (required, exactly one value), cell:subject (required, one or two resource IRIs — e.g. a p:Person/o:Organization — identifying who or what the cell's relationship is about), cell:memberTopics (required, one or more topic:SCTopicGraph containers backing the cell's content — one per member in the relationship, cardinality enforced per member count), cell:otherTopics (any number of additional topic:SCTopicGraph containers beyond that baseline), and cell:shape. Every concrete member-count class (cell:OneMember, cell:TwoMember, cell:ThreePlusMember) is a subclass of cell:ACell, not cell:Cell directly — so an ordinary instantiated cell is typed through its member class alone, and only a cell that is also a template (see cell-templates.ttl) needs cell:TCell added explicitly. A cell is not typed cell:ACell at all until it has real content (cell:memberCount set) — a pure tree-position placeholder with nothing filed under it yet stays a bare cell:Cell, and is therefore exempt from requiring cell:subject/cell:memberTopics."@en .

cell:shape rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "shape"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (non-template) cell:ACell individual directly to the sh:NodeShape(s) validating that specific cell's own content — its own linked topic data — rather than describing what a topic filed under some other, template category should look like (that's cell:templateShape, on cell:TCell). Populated by copy-on-clone: when Lazy Instantiation clones a cat:Category subclass's cell:TCell (via cat:templateCell) into a new cell:ACell for a user's tree, whatever cell:templateShape value the TCell carried is copied into the new ACell's cell:shape — the same validation expectation, now attached to the concrete instance rather than the abstract template, so content later filed under that instantiated cell can actually be validated. Optional; most cells carry no cell:shape value, since most template cells carry no cell:templateShape value to copy in the first place."@en .

cell:creator rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range [ rdf:type owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( p:Person o:Organization ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "creator"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies who created a cell's content — a named individual of p:Person or o:Organization. Required, exactly one value, since every actual cell's content is authored by someone. Mirrors the union-range pattern used by topic:claimant (topic.ttl), referencing those classes by name without owl:imports. Only actual/instantiated cells (cell:ACell) have a creator."@en .

cell:memberCount rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "memberCount"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a cell:ACell individual to the concrete cell:ACell subclass it instantiates: cell:OneMember, cell:TwoMember, or cell:ThreePlusMember. Value is the class itself (e.g. cell:OneMember), not a string — the same class-value-punning pattern cell:origin uses for its own range, cat:Category. Exactly one value required, matching that individual's own rdf:type. Only actual/instantiated cells (cell:ACell) carry member composition."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Member Composition
#
#    An actual (cell:ACell) cell varies by how many total members
#    (the user plus zero or more others) are involved in the
#    relationship it represents: cell:OneMember (the user alone), or
#    cell:MultiMember (the user plus one or more others, further split
#    into cell:TwoMember and cell:ThreePlusMember). Both are subclasses
#    of cell:ACell, not cell:Cell directly.
#################################################################

cell:MultiMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "MultiMember"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Abstract superclass of the two cell types involving more than one total member: cell:TwoMember (exactly one other member) and cell:ThreePlusMember (two or more other members, e.g. a group)."@en .

cell:TwoMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:MultiMember ;
    rdfs:label "TwoMember"@en ;
    cell:label "2 Member Cell" ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell representing a 1:1 relationship with a specific person, organization, or other member."@en .

cell:ThreePlusMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:MultiMember ;
    rdfs:label "ThreePlusMember"@en ;
    cell:label "3+ Member Cell" ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell representing a shared relationship with two or more other members — e.g. several people and/or organizations."@en .

cell:OneMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "OneMember"@en ;
    cell:label "1 Member Cell" ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell with no external member — plain content with no connection or group semantics."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Subject and Topic Links
#
#    cell:subject has domain cell:ACell and is an
#    owl:AnnotationProperty (not an ObjectProperty — see its own
#    comment below); cell:memberTopics and cell:otherTopics both have
#    domain cell:ACell and are owl:ObjectProperty. A cell not yet
#    typed cell:ACell (no real content filed under it yet) carries
#    none of the three.
#
#    cell:memberTopics is the required baseline — at least one
#    topic:SCTopicGraph per member in the relationship (cardinality
#    enforced per member count by cell-shacl.ttl's per-member-class
#    shapes, not expressible as a single OWL cardinality restriction
#    here); cell:otherTopics is any number of additional topics beyond
#    that baseline, uniformly optional and unbounded regardless of
#    member count.
#################################################################

cell:subject rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range xsd:anyURI ;
    rdfs:label "subject"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies the underlying resource(s) — typically a p:Person or o:Organization, e.g. :Bob_Johnson or :Self — that an actual (instantiated) cell's relationship is about. One value for a cell whose content centers on a single member (e.g. :Self alone on a cell:OneMember cell, or the shared entity alone on a cell:ThreePlusMember cell); up to two for a cell:TwoMember cell (:Self and the other member). Required: one or two values (1..2), enforced per member count by cell-shacl.ttl's per-member-class shapes via sh:nodeKind sh:IRI/sh:minCount/sh:maxCount (not expressed as an OWL cardinality restriction here, matching this ontology's convention for cell:memberCount). Distinct from cell:memberTopics/cell:otherTopics, which link to the actual topic:SCTopicGraph DataBook container(s) backing the cell's content — cell:subject identifies *who or what*, cell:memberTopics/cell:otherTopics identify *which topic files*. An owl:AnnotationProperty with range xsd:anyURI, since an owl:ObjectProperty cannot have a datatype range — mirroring topic:subject's identical pattern."@en .

cell:memberTopics rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range topic:SCTopicGraph ;
    rdfs:label "memberTopics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (instantiated) cell to its required baseline of Subject-Claimant classified topic DataBooks (topic:SCTopicGraph, which has no subclasses) — at least one per member in the relationship. Cardinality is enforced per member count by cell-shacl.ttl's per-member-class shapes: exactly 1 for cell:OneMember; 2..4 for cell:TwoMember (matching the four self-vs-other combinations — self-by-self, other-by-self, other-by-other, self-by-other); 3..N for cell:ThreePlusMember (at least one per member, no upper bound). Which of the four self-vs-other cases a given value represents comes from that topic's own subject/claimant combination, not from which property linked it or from a distinct rdf:type."@en .

cell:otherTopics rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range topic:SCTopicGraph ;
    rdfs:label "otherTopics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (instantiated) cell to any number of additional Subject-Claimant classified topic DataBooks (topic:SCTopicGraph) beyond the required cell:memberTopics baseline — e.g. extra notes, historical topics, or supplementary claims not tied to a specific self-vs-other combination. Optional, unbounded (0..N), uniformly across all member counts — enforced by cell-shacl.ttl's :ACellShape (sh:class topic:SCTopicGraph only, no sh:minCount)."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Folders
#
#    Every cell IS its own folder in local storage — not merely
#    associated with one via a stored path. A cell's folder is simply
#    whichever folder its own cell-databook file physically lives in;
#    since a folder holds at most one cell-databook (see the Cell
#    section above), this folder<->cell mapping is always unambiguous,
#    with nothing left to store, detect divergence in, or keep in
#    sync. There is no RDF individual representing that folder at all
#    — the folder is purely a filesystem fact.
#
#    A cell's own content — its folder note and any other arbitrary
#    files — is everything inside that folder EXCEPT:
#      1. the cell-databook file itself;
#      2. the one file named X.md (matching the folder's own name X),
#         recognized by convention as the folder note for that cell —
#         matching the Obsidian Folder Notes plugin convention, and
#         directly compatible with other PKM folder-note tools such
#         as Logseq or Foam; and
#      3. any subfolder that is itself a category folder (i.e.
#         directly contains its own cell-databook file) — that
#         subfolder belongs to a separate, nested cell instead (see
#         the folder ownership boundary rule below).
#    Both (1) and (2) are naming conventions only, discovered by
#    looking inside the folder — neither is tracked by a separate
#    ontology property.
#
#    Folder ownership boundary: a subfolder is excluded from its
#    parent cell's own content — belonging instead to a separate,
#    nested category-tree position of its own — if and only if it
#    directly contains a *.databook.md file. Every category-tree
#    folder already carries such a file — its own cell-databook,
#    whether real content or an empty placeholder — serving double
#    duty as both content holder and tree-node marker; no other marker
#    is needed. This makes ownership resolvable one folder at a time —
#    check what's directly inside a subfolder, not the whole category
#    tree — and robust to the user moving folders around outside Mia,
#    since the marker file travels with whatever folder it's inside;
#    there is no separately recorded path to reconcile against.
#
#    Because a cell's own note and its other files share one folder,
#    the single Cells root can be opened directly as a PKM vault
#    (e.g. an Obsidian vault) without hiding anything from it: modern
#    PKM tools handle non-Markdown files natively, so there is no need
#    for a second, notes-only hierarchy kept separate from arbitrary
#    files.
#################################################################

cell:origin rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:range cat:Category ;
    rdfs:label "origin"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The cat:Category subclass this cell was originally instantiated as — the concrete leaf class itself (e.g. cat:Others, cat:BankingPayments), not a string, mirroring cell:memberCount's use of class-value punning as an object property's range. For one of the four templated classes (cat:Passport/cat:BirthCertificate/cat:DriversLicense/cat:MedicalAppointmentInfo), this is literally the class whose cell:TCell template was cloned into this cell (Lazy Instantiation, via category.ttl's cat:templateCell); for any other cell, it is simply the category the cell was created to represent, asserted directly with no template involved. At most one value (0..1), on any cell:Cell regardless of facet, matching cell.png's origin arrow off Cell. Absent exactly when the cell is of the UserDefined category — a category folder the user created without picking any existing cat:Category class, identified precisely by its cell-databook's filename carrying the literal (custom) disambiguator in place of an origin-derived <catType> (e.g. Friends(custom).databook.md), not by a vague 'no canonical counterpart' judgment call. This is a different fact from whether the folder's own name matches its origin's rdfs:label verbatim — see the green ('Predefined')/purple ('UserDefined') folder-name-text convention documented in README.md and CLAUDE.md's Check 15/10j, which only applies when an origin IS present; a UserDefined (no-origin) cell's name is trivially always 'UserDefined' text too, since there is no label to possibly match. This value is fixed at the point the cell was created — it is not re-derived from the folder's current name, so it needs no update if the folder is later moved or renamed, keeping a cell:Cell robust under PDN sync exactly as its own class comment already promises (no link to a tree position). Purpose: when a cell is shared with another PDN member, the recipient's Mia app can use this value, if present, as a hint for which folder in the recipient's own tree the incoming cell should be filed under — a snapshot of the sender's original classification, robust to either side's tree being organized differently. A folder's own filesystem position has no RDF representation at all — UserDefined is a filename/display convention only, never an OWL class. Range cat:Category is referenced by name without owl:imports, mirroring cell:creator's identical pattern for p:Person/o:Organization (persona.ttl/organization.ttl) — category.ttl already imports cell.ttl, so importing it back here would create a mutual import for no reason cell:origin actually needs."@en .
